<P><B>Chart 4: <i>kalle�al</i> (distribution & syntactic roles)</B>

<P>The first item, <i>kalle�</i>, is an ad-nominal (or adjectival)
form<A HREF="#fn34">(34)</A> that we find
in examples like:<A HREF="#fn35">(35)</A>
<P><B>(5)</B> <i>kalle� ci�uku�i</i>
<BR>say-KAL_ZERO small-hamlet
<BR>[AK, 110-13] "little hamlet of great bustle" (VMS[1975)

<P><B>(6)</B> <i>kalle� k�attu</i>
<BR>say-KAL_ZERO forest_Obl.
<BR>[KU, 179-1] "[...] noisy forest" (Hart[1975])


<P>Coming to the predicative forms, <i>kalle��a</i> (plural) and
<i>kalle��a��u</i> (singular), I will cite one <i>Ku�untoki</i> example,
reduced of course to the bare bones, along with two of its
published translations:
<P><B>(7)</B> <i>ko�iy�r n� [...] kalle��a</i> [KU, 24-6]
<BR>cruel_Pl.Hum. tongue say-KAL_Pl.Neut.
<BR>"These cruel women's tongues are working on me" (A.K.
Ramanujan[1967])
<BR>"The tongues of these evil people are pounding me"
(Shanmugam Pilli & David Ludden [1976])

<P>I will not give here an example for the neutral singular
predicative form <i>kalle��ୟu</i>, but to conclude this exploration of
syntactic positions, I will give two examples for <i>kalle�a</i>, the
converb<A HREF="#fn36">(36)</A> or ad-verbal
participle: the first one illustrates its adverbial role, and the
second illustrates its absolutive role.
<P><B>(8)</B> <i>[...] kalle�a olittu [...]</i> (PU, 320-9)
<BR>say-KAL_CONV1. resound_CONV2
<BR>"cackling loudly" (Hart[1999])

<P><B>(9)</B> <i>[...] peru� ka�ip p�kkam kalle�a, varum [...] ko�ka� t�r
[...]</i>
<BR>big backwater maritime-village say-KAL_CONV1
come_NON-PAST.3rd maritime-chief chariot
<BR>"The chariot of the maritime chief will come, making
vociferous the wide marshy tract." (N.Kandaswamy
Pilli[1970])

<P>We should add to these observations that, as noted in 3.4., not
every ideophone can be seen in the corpus under examination to
have all the syntactic roles. Many ideophones have a preferred
syntactic role. If we take for instance <i>�er�re�al</i>, which is item
I-16, we see that it can occur only in an adverbial role, like in
the following passage, excerpted from the description of a
marriage ceremony:

<P><B>(10)</B> <i>kalle� cummiy�r �er�re�a pukutantu</i> (AK)
<BR>say-KAL_ZERO noisy-ones say-�ER�R_CONV1 enter_CONV2
<BR>"[the relations hand her over], entering in haste [into the
marriage house] with tumultuous noise" (VMS[1975])

<HR>

<A NAME="TOC_4"><H3>4. CONCLUSIONS: EVOLUTION OF THE SET OF IDEOPHONES</H3>

<P><B>4.1.</B> We now hold in our hands both ends of the story of
ideophones, one being the modern period with example (2) and the
use of <i>di��r-��u</i> (See 1.2.), the other one being the most ancient
period reachable by us, the so-called Sangam period, with
examples (5) to (10) illustrating the use of <i>kalle�al</i> and
<i>�er�re�al</i>. There would remin for us the task to examine what is
to be observed between these two ends.
Is there any continuity? As I have already remarked
once, the focus must not necessarily lie on the individual items,
but it could also be brought on the groups and subgroups of
items, on the suffixes, on the expressive devices (like for
instance the reduplication), that is to say on the dynamics that
is seen in them.

<P>If we examine for instance the data presented in Chart 2, in the
light of the elements that were seen in Chart 1, it can be seen
that among the 23 ideophones present in Sangam Tamil, and if we
concern ourselves only with those mentioned in MTL:
<UL>
<LI> All belong to the S group
<LI> 14 belong to subgroup {1a}: <i>kalle�al</i>,
<i>pulle�al</i>, <i>ta��e�al</i>, etc.
<LI> 3, with <i>-um</i> suffix, belong to subgroup {1b}:
<i>i�ume�al</i>, etc.
<LI> 1 belongs to subgroup {1c}: <i>�er�re�al</i>.
</UL>

<P><B>4.2.</B> The testimony of an ancient lexicon

<P>It is, in this connection, interesting to have a look at the
testimony that is provided by two traditionnal (non-alphabetical)
lexicons, the <i>Tiv�karam</i>, probably dating back to the VIIIth
century<A HREF="#fn37">(37)</A>, and the <i>C���ma�i
Nika��u</i>, dating back to ca. 1520 AD. The interesting fact is that
one of the verses in <i>Tiv�karam</i> contins a short list of 8
ideophones. This is an interesting evidence that the linguistic
class which I have been trying to describe, whatever be the name
used to designate it, was having a psychological reality or an
intuitive relevance for (ancient) speakers of Ancient Tamil, or
at least for such ancient knowledgeable speakers as those who
made and used these lexicons.<A HREF="#fn38">(38)</A>

<P>The 8 items enumerated by verse 1900<A HREF="#fn39">(39)</A> of
<i>Tiv�karam</i> are the following:
<UL>
<LI>1. <i>imme�al</i> (I-7, MTL014)
<LI>2. <i>kalle�al</i> (I-1, MTL062)
<LI>3. <i>i�ume�al</i> (I-8, MTL017)
<LI>4. <i>valle�al</i> (I-15, MTL536)
<LI>5. <i>pomme�al</i> (MTL465)
<LI>6. <i>olle�al</i> (I-9, MTL031)
<LI>7. <i>po��e�al</i> (MTL473)
<LI>8. <i>�e��e�al</i> (MTL255)
</UL>

<P>We see that five of them (I-1, I-7, I-8, I-9 & I-15) appear
indeed inside the most ancient part of Tamil literature. Of the
remining three, <i>po��e�al</i> is already attested in <i>Ku�a�</i> 487. It
can as well be found in C�vakacint�ma�i, where we also see
<i>pomme�al</i> (equally attested in <i>Tirukk�viy�r</i>). But a search for
the last item, <i>�e��e�al</i>, has remined unfruitful,<A HREF="#fn40">(40)</A>
and the only authority that MTL is able to quote for
it, is <i>Tiv�karam</i> itself, which explins the briefness of its
explination: "an imitative sound".

<P><B>4.3.</B> The wider indian context
The explination found inside MTL concerning <i>�e��e�al</i> is not
based on the western reflection on the onomatopoeia and the
"expressive" symbolism which Apte presented mentioning Sapir (see
1.3.). As far as MTL is concerned, he is simply translating the
verse from <i>Tiv�karam</i>, that reads thus (in the reference edition):
<P><i>imme�al, kalle�al, i�ume�al, valle�al, pomme�al, olle�al,
po��e�al, �e��e�al, a��a a�ukara�a �ci �kum</i>

<P>This verse says that the 8 words are (instances of) <i>anukara�a
�ci</i>. It is of course not possible in the present circumstance to
explore here all the ramifications of that fact, and I
will content myself with simply remarking that <i>anukara�a</i>, which
seems to be the source for the "imitative word" of MTL, is a
sanskrit term found in P��ini's grammar
in three s�tra-s<A HREF="#fn41">(41)</A>, and that
these s�tra-s seem to deal with what might be ideophones in
sanskrit.<A HREF="#fn42">(42)</A>

<P><B>4.4.</B> How the story continues.

<P>We now come back to <i>Tiv�karam</i>. As seen in the 1990-1993 critical
edition, the list of eight items mentioned in 4.2. is what is
seen in manuscripts. However, the first edition of Tiv�karam,
published in 1839, by T���avar�ya Mutaliy�r, added as a followup
to the list of eight items two more lists:
<UL>
<LI> one of those mentioned 6 items: <i>komme�al</i>, <i>vi�le�al</i>,
<i>ter�le�al</i>,<A HREF="#fn43">(43)</A>
<i>muk�re�al</i>, <i>amme�al</i>, <i>car�le�al</i>.
<LI> the second, added 10 more items, all from the R(eduplicative)
group (see 2.4): <i>pa�apa�e�al</i>, <i>ka�aka�e�al</i>, <i>patapate�al</i>,
<i>ko�ako�e�al</i>, <i>mo�umo�e�al</i>, <i>ti�uti�e�al</i>, <i>mokamoke�al</i>,
<i>kalakale�al</i>, <i>ne�ane�e�al</i>, <i>ne�ane�e�al</i>, <i>ka�aka�e�al</i>. The
technical term designating those last is <i>upaya-v-�ci</i>, where we
agin see one sanskrit component <i>upaya</i> "double".
</UL>

<P>The explination for this is that T���avar�ya Mutaliy�r has
"improved" the <i>Tiv�karam</i> text by making use of a more recent
lexicon. The triple list is in fact found inside the <i>C���ma�i
Nika��u</i> (voir �dition App�c�mi Mutaliy�r, kur�ti varu�am). But by
doing this, he has of course created some temporal
inconsistencies, becuse VIIIth century vocabulary is not the
same as that which is found in the XVIth century.

<P>If we re-attribute the second and the third list just mentioned
to the correct periods, we see that they show
<UL>
<LI>that the real development of the {1c} group, which was attested
only by the I-16 item (<i>�er�re�al</i>) in the ancient period took
probably place after the <i>Tiv�karam</i>
<LI>that the massive development of the R group, which is not
attested at all in the ancient period,<A HREF="#fn44">(44)</A>
took also place during the same period.
</UL>

<P>It will be my task, in some forthcoming studies, to try to throw
more light on the precise evolution of this small corner of the
Tamil vocabulary.

