But this is not the case for all the 29 items listed by Asher.

<P><A NAME="fn10">(10) I
shall not attempt here to establish a usage distinction between
these two forms. The 2 examples given by MTL, under 2 different
meanings, are: <i>ti��re�a vant�</i> "unexpectedly he-came" and <i>ti��re��u vi�untatu</i> "<i>ti��re��u</i> it-fell",
where <i>ti��r-e��u</i> is sid
by MTL to be an onom. qualifying the act of falling, in terms of
<i>oli</i> (sound).

<P><A NAME="fn11">(11) In
the final version of this study, an attempt may be made, on the
line of Steever[1983] (chapter 1) commenting on Benv�niste and on
Bloch, to ascertin more precisely the characteristics of the
quotative verb <i>e�al</i> in its "auxiliant" role inside the <i>X-e�al</i>
expressions that are studied here.


<P><A NAME="fn12">(12) However, I shall concentrate
in this article on a set of forms which is homogeneous
in an inflectional sense, and shall
not try to see things from the point of view of the root as
Malten does.


<P><A NAME="fn13">(13) This is evidently an impossible task, but this is
